#
# Rebuild the registered modules against the kernel an update has just fetched, before the host
# reboots onto it. scripts/firmware/upgrade.sh runs this level between installing the packages and
# applying the pending kernel, so the kernel is still a staged set here; follow.sh reads it there.
#
# It never fails an update: every path exits 0, and a failure is a warning in the log under
# kernelfollow. With nothing registered, follow.sh returns at once.
#
# Bounded since 2026-10-29, as the stop hook always was: half an hour - the fifteen minutes a run can
# wait for another's lock, then a fetch and a build - so that a fetch that will not finish cannot hold
# System: Firmware's upgrade with nothing on its screen. The stop hook tries again at the reboot that
# follows, and the five-minute run after it.
FOLLOW=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/kernelfollow/follow.sh
: ${LIMIT:=1800}
[ -f "${FOLLOW}" ] || exit 0
if ! OUT=$(/usr/bin/timeout -k 10 "${LIMIT}" /bin/sh "${FOLLOW}" 2>&1); then
	/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err \
	    "a module could NOT be rebuilt for the kernel this update installed, or not within ${LIMIT}s"
	echo "${OUT}" | /usr/bin/tail -8 | while read -r l; do
		/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err "  ${l}"
	done
fi
exit 0
